body:
Progress: `add.s` now passes (plain DP fixed). Two remaining words from the A32 corpus, with the exact snippets:

1) `mov r2,r0,lsl r1` = e1a02110. bit4=1, bits24-23=11, so the current `(op & 0x18)==0x18 -> arm_mem` sends it to arm_mem; arm_mem correctly returns UNDEF. This is V10 data processing register-shifted-register. `/tmp/n2hw-T01/asmrun/rs.s` stops there.
2) `ldrh r2,[r0,#-2]` with r0=0x30002 = e15020b2 (P=0/U=1). bit4=1, bits24-23=01, so the current test misses it and it falls to arm_dp; `/tmp/n2hw-T01/asmrun/lh2.s` stops there. (e1d020b0, P=1, happens to have bits24-23=11 and works.)

Both are fixed by the 11:52 consolidated hunk: multiply mask first, then `(insn & 0x90)==0x90` with the bits7-4 nibble test (B/D/F, or 9 with bits11-8=F), then the exact QADD/SMLA masks. I just re-ran it as /tmp/n2hw-T01/exp2/core_fix5.c: add.s, rs.s, lh2.s all stop=ok with correct values; the 97-kernel arm-O0 corpus has zero non-VFP stops. If you prefer a smaller step: replace only the `(op & 0x18)==0x18` test with

  if ((insn & 0x90u) == 0x90u) {
      unsigned lo = (insn >> 4) & 0xFu;
      if (lo == 0xBu || lo == 0xDu || lo == 0xFu ||
          (lo == 0x9u && ((insn >> 8) & 0xFu) == 0xFu))
          return n2a32_arm_mem;
  }

and add the multiply `(insn & 0x0F0000F0)==0x90 -> arm_dp` before it (MUL has bit4=1/bit7=1 too). That is the whole difference.
